# Agent Development Manifest

This file defines how agents must work in this project.

## Context System

- `project_context` is an Obsidian vault and the canonical project context layer.
- Before implementation, read [Context index](00%20Context%20Index.md), [Agent context protocol](Agent%20Context%20Protocol.md), [Project overview](Project%20Overview.md), [Architecture map](Architecture%20Map.md), and [Implementation notes](Implementation%20Notes.md).
- Treat `.obsidian` as vault configuration. Do not delete or rewrite `.obsidian` files unless the task is explicitly about Obsidian configuration.
- Use `project_context` as the memory store for the project. Verify the vault and the source files together before editing behavior.
- Keep Obsidian notes, context documents, and code references synchronized during implementation.
- If source code and context disagree, inspect source code first, then update the context note or record the mismatch in [Context changelog](Context%20Changelog.md).
- When documentation mentions concrete implementation, link to the relevant source file, for example [useGame.ts](../src/useGame.ts), [gameCore.ts](../src/gameCore.ts), [gameChallenge.ts](../src/gameChallenge.ts), [highScoreStorage.ts](../src/highScoreStorage.ts), [Scene.tsx](../src/Scene.tsx), [App.tsx](../src/App.tsx), [gameConstants.ts](../src/gameConstants.ts), [viewMath.ts](../src/viewMath.ts), [gameMetrics.ts](../src/gameMetrics.ts), [gameMetricsStorage.ts](../src/gameMetricsStorage.ts), [gameReplay.ts](../src/gameReplay.ts), or [audio.ts](../src/audio.ts).
- Maintain bidirectional context links where practical: new stable context notes should be linked from [Context index](00%20Context%20Index.md), and affected notes should link back to related project docs or code.
- Meaningful context changes must include a versioned entry in [Context changelog](Context%20Changelog.md).

## Project Principles

- Preserve the current stack unless the user explicitly asks for a migration: React, Vite, Tailwind CSS, Three.js, React Three Fiber.
- Treat this as a game project, not a generic React dashboard.
- Keep the playfield readable, responsive, and fast.
- Prefer deterministic game rules over visual-only fixes.
- Do not hide technical debt behind UI polish.
- Treat context versioning as part of the implementation surface, not an afterthought.

## Architecture Rules

- Separate simulation from rendering.
- Core gameplay rules must not depend on React, R3F, DOM, Web Audio, storage, or analytics.
- React state is for coarse UI state. High-frequency simulation data should live in refs, a simulation object, or a dedicated store.
- Rendering components should receive a narrow view model and avoid owning gameplay rules.
- Input must be mapped to semantic actions: `move`, `pause`, `restart`, `toggleDevMode`, `useAbility`.
- Audio, persistence, analytics, and dev tools are adapters around the game core.
- Avoid centralizing every feature in `useGame.ts`. Extract pure modules when rules become testable.

## Testing Rules

- Do not use Playwright or browser tests when the task explicitly forbids them.
- For gameplay changes, add or update unit tests before considering the change complete.
- Required unit-test areas:
  - row and column mapping;
  - movement bounds and snapping;
  - road collision;
  - river platform support;
  - goal trigger rules;
  - scoring;
  - lives and game-over transitions;
  - level generation constraints;
  - developer-mode overrides.
- Every physics or collision change must include positive and negative cases.
- Keep tests deterministic. Use seeded data and explicit state snapshots.

## UI and Visual Rules

- DOM is the default layer for HUD, menus, buttons, pause, settings, and text-heavy UI.
- R3F/Three.js is the default layer for the playfield, characters, obstacles, environment, particles, and camera.
- Protect the center and lower-middle playfield during normal gameplay.
- Keep persistent HUD compact.
- Support compact mobile and desktop layouts explicitly.
- Do not rely on emoji for critical status communication when a controlled icon or asset is feasible.
- Use design constants for lane heights, platform heights, frog offsets, shadows, colors, and effect durations.
- Avoid one-off inline styles when a reusable component or CSS variable is appropriate.

## Performance Rules

- Avoid per-frame React state updates for entity positions.
- Avoid per-frame allocation of large arrays or entity objects in hot paths.
- Reuse Three.js materials and geometries where practical.
- Prefer instancing or pooled meshes for repeated decoration.
- Keep water, particle, lighting, and shadow costs bounded.
- Monitor bundle size because the project uses single-file Vite output.

## Gameplay Rules

- The primary loop is: move frog, avoid road hazards, ride river platforms, reach unique lily pads, complete all goals, advance level.
- Any new mechanic must preserve this loop unless explicitly scoped as a mode.
- Difficulty changes must be explainable through rules: speed, lane count, platform spacing, hazard density, modifiers, or objectives.
- Bonus abilities must be temporary, visible, testable, and reversible.
- Game-over and restart behavior must be explicit actions, not synthetic keyboard events.

## Developer Mode Rules

- Developer mode must never silently affect production behavior.
- Developer mode flags must be explicit and test-covered.
- Useful flags include invulnerability, disabled collision, infinite lives, speed scale, level jump, force goal, force death, collision-box display, and step simulation.
- Debug UI must not obscure normal play unless explicitly enabled.

## Change Hygiene

- Read the surrounding code before editing.
- Read and reconcile the Obsidian context before editing.
- Keep edits scoped to the requested behavior.
- Do not rewrite architecture opportunistically while fixing a narrow bug.
- Do not remove user changes unless the user explicitly requests it.
- Prefer small pure functions for rules that need tests.
- Document new rules in `project_context` when they affect gameplay, testing, or agent behavior.
- Document stable decisions in `project_context` when they affect architecture, gameplay, testing, visuals, developer mode, or agent workflow.
- Keep context documentation versioned through [Context changelog](Context%20Changelog.md).

## Completion Criteria

A change is complete only when:

- the implementation matches the requested behavior;
- Obsidian context has been checked and updated when relevant;
- documentation links point to mentioned files, code, and related context notes;
- relevant unit tests exist or the reason for missing tests is documented;
- `npm run build` passes unless the task is documentation-only and build is not relevant;
- no browser or Playwright tests were used when prohibited;
- new technical debt is recorded instead of hidden;
- user-facing behavior and developer-mode behavior remain clearly separated.
